<!--
    Library-level color resources for LayeredEditors.Avalonia.

    The six token colors are theme-neutral (they work on both Semi Light and Dark
    backgrounds); the accent PAIR is per-variant, for the reason given below.
    Host applications can override any key in their own Application.Resources
    before the first render — no library fork required.  An override of a
    per-variant key belongs in the host's own ThemeDictionaries.

    Included automatically by SemiBundle.axaml via StyleInclude. Hosts that use a
    different theme must include this file explicitly if they want the defaults:

        <StyleInclude Source="avares://LayeredEditors.Avalonia/Themes/EditorColors.axaml" />

    WHY THIS IS A <Styles> FILE AND NOT A <ResourceDictionary>
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Avalonia's XAML compiler (XamlX) compiles .axaml to IL at build time, so type
    mismatches become hard build errors (AVLN3000) rather than runtime surprises.

    The first attempt used a <ResourceDictionary> here and included it from
    SemiBundle.axaml via:

        <Styles.Resources>
            <ResourceInclude Source="...EditorColors.axaml" />
        </Styles.Resources>

    XamlX rejected that with AVLN3000: Styles.Resources is an IResourceDictionary
    (IDictionary<object,object>) — it accepts key-value brush entries directly, but
    not a ResourceInclude node (which is an IResourceProvider, not a KVP).

    The fix: declare this file as a <Styles> document so SemiBundle.axaml can pull
    it in with a plain <StyleInclude>, which is the idiomatic Avalonia 11 pattern
    for composing theme fragments.

    WHY THE ACCENT PAIR IS PER-VARIANT AND THE REST IS NOT
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The other six are foregrounds picked to stay legible on either ground, which
    a single mid-tone can do.  The accent pair is a PILL — text measured against
    its own tint — and the badge that uses it is 9px bold, so the floor is 4.5:1
    with no AA Large exemption.  No single color can clear 4.5:1 against both a
    white page and a near-black one: that needs relative luminance <= 0.183 for
    the light ground and >= 0.215 for the dark, which is a contradiction.  Hence
    a Light/Dark pair, which is also what lets the pill sit a fixed, deliberate
    step above whichever page it lands on.
-->
<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <Styles.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
                <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
                    <!-- PropertyEditorWrapper — accent tint pill (the "✨ NEW" badge):
                         a tinted background plus matching-hue text, NOT white on a
                         saturated fill.  The badge annotates the property-name heading
                         beside it, so it has to read lighter than what it annotates.
                         Text #0F4C81 on #DCEAF7 = 7.24:1; pill 1.22:1 above a white page. -->
                    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.AccentBrush" Color="#0F4C81" />
                    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.AccentBackgroundBrush" Color="#DCEAF7" />
                </ResourceDictionary>
                <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
                    <!-- Dark counterpart: text #9BCFF2 on #152C42 = 8.57:1;
                         pill 1.27:1 above a #161618 page. -->
                    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.AccentBrush" Color="#9BCFF2" />
                    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.AccentBackgroundBrush" Color="#152C42" />
                </ResourceDictionary>
            </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

            <!-- LinkifiedTextBlock — URL and env-var token colors -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.LinkBrush" Color="#006B8A" />  <!-- teal-blue: unvisited URL -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.VisitedBrush" Color="#6B3FA0" />  <!-- purple: visited URL -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.EnvVarBrush" Color="#1565C0" />  <!-- blue: env-var token -->

            <!-- BoolToStatusBrushConverter — tri-state boolean indicator colors -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.BoolEnabled" Color="#2E7D32" />  <!-- green: true / enabled -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.BoolDisabled" Color="#C62828" />  <!-- red: false / disabled -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="LE.BoolUnset" Color="#9E9E9E" />  <!-- grey: null / unset -->
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Styles.Resources>

</Styles>
